            <ArticleTitle>[Adnexal torsion: a report of 135 cases].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>535-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the clinical findings and therapeutic options following adnexal torsion.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A retrospective series of 135 patients presenting with adnexal torsion between January 1992 and December 2001 in Sousse- Tunisia- a university teaching hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Adnexal torsion accounted for 14.8% of surgically treated adnexal tumors. Pregnancy was associated in 17% of cases and diagnosis was accurate at first clinical examination in only 57.8% of cases. Ultrasound allowed visualisation of Adnexal Diseases in all cases. Operative laparoscopy was successfully achieved in 34% of patients. Conservative management was used in 57% of the premenopausal patients. No major postoperative complication was noted.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Clinicians must be aware of a possible adnexal torsion in women with acute pelvic pain. Ultrasound is a useful tool in these situations. Laparoscopy allows diagnosis and surgical management even during pregnancy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
